    {
      "name": "Technical",
      "num_qs": 15,
      "marks": 30,
      "question": [
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270720",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following languages is not Turing-recognizable?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$\\{\\langle M\\rangle \\mid &nbsp;M &nbsp;\\text{ is a deterministic Turing machine and } M \\text{&nbsp;accepts }010 \\}$</li>\n    <li>$\\{\\langle M\\rangle \\mid &nbsp;M \\text{ is a non deterministic Turing machine and } M&nbsp;\\text{ accepts }010 \\}$</li>\n    <li>$\\{\\langle M\\rangle \\mid &nbsp;M \\text{ is a deterministic Turing machine and } L(M) \\neq \\{\\} \\}$</li>\n    <li>$\\{\\langle M\\rangle \\mid &nbsp;M \\text{ is a deterministic Turing machine and } L(M) =&nbsp;\\Sigma^*&nbsp;\\}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,recursive-and-recursively-enumerable-languages",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270719",
          "text": "<p>Which one of the following statements is FALSE?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>All regular languages are Turing decidable</li>\n    <li>If&nbsp;$L$&nbsp;and $\\bar L$&nbsp;are Turing recognizable then $L$ is Turing decidable</li>\n    <li>If&nbsp;$L_1$&nbsp;is regular and&nbsp;$L_2$&nbsp;is Turing decidable then&nbsp;$L_1\\cap L_2$&nbsp;is Turing decidable</li>\n    <li>The language&nbsp;$\\{\\langle M\\rangle \\mid&nbsp;M$&nbsp;is finite or infinite&nbsp;$\\}$&nbsp;is undecidable</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,recursive-and-recursively-enumerable-languages",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270718",
          "text": "<p>Consider the Turing Machine $M$ over the input alphabet&nbsp;$\\Sigma&nbsp;= \\{0, 1\\}$ and&nbsp;$Γ&nbsp;= \\{0, 1, x, y,⊔\\},$ described by the following diagram. The $\\text{REJECT}$ state&nbsp;$q_{rej}$&nbsp;is not shown, and all \"missing\" transitions are assumed to go to the $\\text{REJECT}$ state.<br>\n<img alt=\"GATEBOOK-2019-TOC3-3\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is FALSE?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$0111\\text{ is not accepted}$</li>\n    <li>$000111&nbsp;\\text{ is accepted}$</li>\n    <li>$L(M) = \\{0^n1^n&nbsp;\\mid&nbsp;n\\geq 0\\}$</li>\n    <li>$\\delta (q_2, x) = (q_0, x, R)$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,turing-machine",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270717",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following statements:</p>\n\n<ul>\n    <li>S1: For any $k &gt; 1,$ there is no language that is decided by a TM with $k$ tapes, but is undecidable by any TM having $k − 1$ or less tapes.</li>\n    <li>S2: If a language&nbsp;$L$&nbsp;is context-free then&nbsp;$\\bar L$&nbsp;is TM decidable.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>&nbsp; &nbsp; Which of the above statements is TRUE?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only S1</li>\n    <li>Only S2</li>\n    <li>Both S1 and S2</li>\n    <li>None</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,recursive-and-recursively-enumerable-languages",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270716",
          "text": "<p>$L&nbsp;=&nbsp;\\{x_1 \\#&nbsp;x_2 \\# \\ldots \\#x_n&nbsp;\\mid&nbsp;x_i \\in \\{a,b\\}^*&nbsp;\\}$&nbsp;for each&nbsp;$i$&nbsp;and, for some&nbsp;$i,$&nbsp;$x_i$&nbsp;is a palindrome$\\}.$ $L$ is</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Recursive but not context-free</li>\n    <li>Context free but not regular</li>\n    <li>Recursively enumerable but not recursive</li>\n    <li>Not Recursively enumerable</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,identify-class-language",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270715",
          "text": "<ul>\n    <li>$L=\\{\\langle G,D \\rangle \\mid G$&nbsp;is a CFG and&nbsp;$D$&nbsp;is a DFA&nbsp;and&nbsp;$L(G)\\subseteq L(D)\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements about $L$ is TRUE?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$L$ is context-free&nbsp;but not regular</li>\n    <li>$L$ is regular</li>\n    <li>$L$ is recursive but not context-free</li>\n    <li>$L$ is Recursively enumerable but not recursive</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,recursive-and-recursively-enumerable-languages",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270714",
          "text": "<ul>\n    <li>$L_1=\\{\\langle M\\rangle \\mid M&nbsp;\\text{&nbsp;is a TM and if we start }M$&nbsp;with a blank input tape, it will finally write some non-blank symbol on its tape&nbsp;$\\}$</li>\n    <li>$L_2=\\{\\langle M\\rangle \\mid M$&nbsp;is a TM and accepts a string of odd length&nbsp;$\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$L_1$&nbsp;is decidable but&nbsp;$L_2$&nbsp;is undecidable</li>\n    <li>$L_1$&nbsp;is undecidable but&nbsp;$L_2$&nbsp;is decidable</li>\n    <li>$L_1$&nbsp;is decidable and&nbsp;$L_2$&nbsp;is decidable&nbsp; &nbsp;</li>\n    <li>$L_1$&nbsp;is undecidable and&nbsp;$L_2$&nbsp;is undecidable</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,recursive-and-recursively-enumerable-languages",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270713",
          "text": "<p>Let&nbsp;$L=\\{\\langle M\\rangle \\mid M$&nbsp;is a TM and&nbsp;$L(M)$&nbsp;has at least $23456$ strings&nbsp;$\\}$</p>\n\n<p>Which one of the following statements is False?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$L$&nbsp;is undecidable</li>\n    <li>$L$&nbsp;is recursively enumerable</li>\n    <li>$\\bar L$&nbsp;is recursively enumerable</li>\n    <li>Strings in $L$ can be enumerated by some Turing Machine</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270712",
          "text": "<p>If a language&nbsp;$L$&nbsp;is decidable then which of the statements below is/are true?</p>\n\n<p>P:&nbsp;$L$&nbsp;and&nbsp;$\\bar L$&nbsp;are recursively enumerable</p>\n\n<p>Q:&nbsp;$L$&nbsp;is recursive</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only P</li>\n    <li>Only Q</li>\n    <li>Both P and Q</li>\n    <li>None</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270711",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following language.</p>\n\n<ul>\n    <li>$L_c=\\{⟨M,\\omega⟩\\mid&nbsp;M$&nbsp;does not accept&nbsp;$\\omega\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>$M\\;-$ encoding of Turing machine using&nbsp;the alphabet set $\\Sigma, $<br>\n$\\omega\\;-$ a string of $\\Sigma^*$</p>\n\n<p>$L_c$ is</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Recursive</li>\n    <li>Recursively enumerable</li>\n    <li>Not recursively enumerable</li>\n    <li>Can not be decided</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,recursive-and-recursively-enumerable-languages",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270710",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following Turing Machine:&nbsp;$M=\\left(\\{q_0,q_1,q_2,q_{acc},q_{rej}\\},\\{0,1\\},\\{0,1,t\\},\\delta ,q_0,q_{acc},_{qrej}\\right),$ where</p>\n\n<ul>\n    <li>$\\delta(q_0,0)=(q_1,1,R)$</li>\n    <li>$\\delta(q_1,1)=(q_2,1,L)$</li>\n    <li>$\\delta(q_2,1)=(q_1,1,R)$</li>\n    <li>$\\delta(q_1,t)=(q_{acc},t,R)$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Assume for cases not mentioned above,&nbsp;$δ(q,a)=(q_{rej},t,R)$</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is TRUE about Turing machine $M$?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$M$ halts on all inputs.</li>\n    <li>$M$ never halts on some inputs.</li>\n    <li>$M$ does not halt on any input.</li>\n    <li>Suppose the current configuration is&nbsp;$1q00.$&nbsp;The next configuration is&nbsp;$q111⊔$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,turing-machine",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270709",
          "text": "<p>Suppose&nbsp;$M_1$&nbsp;and&nbsp;$M_2$&nbsp;are two Turing machines such that&nbsp;$L(M_1)\\subseteq L(M_2).$&nbsp;Then</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>On every input on which&nbsp;$M_1$&nbsp;does not halt,&nbsp;$M_2$&nbsp;does not halt</li>\n    <li>On every input on which&nbsp;$M_1$&nbsp;halts,&nbsp;$M_2$&nbsp;halts too</li>\n    <li>On every input which&nbsp;$M_1$&nbsp;accepts,&nbsp;$M_2$&nbsp;halts</li>\n    <li>On every input which&nbsp;$M_2$&nbsp;accepts,&nbsp;$M_1$&nbsp;halts</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,turing-machine",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270708",
          "text": "<p>Suppose&nbsp;$A$&nbsp;and&nbsp;$B$&nbsp;are recursively enumerable languages such that&nbsp;$A\\cup B=\\Sigma^*.$ Further, suppose $(A\\cap \\bar B)\\cup (\\bar A\\cap B)$&nbsp;is decidable. Which of the following is true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>It is possible that either $A$ or $B$ is decidable but not both</li>\n    <li>None of them can be decidable</li>\n    <li>Both of them are regular</li>\n    <li>Both of them are decidable</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,recursive-and-recursively-enumerable-languages,decidability",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270707",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following is a property of recursively enumerable languages?</p>\n\n<ul>\n    <li>$P: \\{M \\mid M \\text{ accepts 312929&nbsp;strings} \\}$</li>\n    <li>$Q:&nbsp;\\{M \\mid M \\text{&nbsp;has 312929&nbsp;states} \\}$</li>\n    <li>$R:&nbsp;\\{M \\mid M \\text{&nbsp;has 312929 symbols in tape alphabet} \\}$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Only $P$</li>\n    <li>Only $P$ and $Q$</li>\n    <li>Only $P$ and $R$</li>\n    <li>All of $P$, $Q$ and $R$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,recursive-and-recursively-enumerable-languages",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "270706",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following language:</p>\n\n<ul>\n    <li>$L=&nbsp;\\{M\\mid&nbsp;M \\text{ is a TM and } \\mid L(M)\\mid \\; \\leq 3&nbsp;\\}$</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>Which of the following statements is true about $L$?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$L$ is recursive but not context free</li>\n    <li>$L$ is recursively enumerable but not recursive&nbsp; &nbsp;</li>\n    <li>$L$ is not recursively enumerable</li>\n    <li>$L$ is regular</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "gb2019-toc3,recursive-and-recursively-enumerable-languages",
          "category": "Theory of Computation"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}